"Jeremy Turner" <turnerjer0000@i_like_to_pet_friendly_goats.com> wrote:

> On 06 Jul 2004, Kent Paul Dolan said:

>> It also offends my inner dungeon explorer that
>> the levels are rectangular instead of square (or
>> better, circular) in aspect; that really klunks
>> and stands out as an artifact left over from
>> earlier screen size limits and screen aspect
>> ratios.

> could you elaborate on this paragraph?

I don't find it the least bit reasonable that the
PC should have immediate knowledge of the whole
level at once, that is grossly unnatural, and the
player having that knowledge is then equally much an
artifact.

If I'm too absent-minded to remember which way it is
from the current PC location to the upstairs (and I
am, often, at my age and in my advancing state of
senility), my PC, role playing going both ways,
should have to behave just as I would, wandering
around blunderingly until a stairwell is re-found.

I'm currently playing with a 48x48 sized tile set
derived from the Absurd tile set, which lets me see
a little more than the room my PC is currently
exploring, but not much more; mostly this is a
deliberate attempt to limit what I as the player and
the surrogate for the PC can see at any one time.

Once you get away from the idea that the whole
dungeon level should _fit_ on the screen, having it
be _shaped_ like the screen instead of some nice
sensible equal dimensioned blobby square or circular
thingie seems like design laziness.

> isn't this because our monitors are rectangular?
> or did they invent round ones while i wasn't
> looking?

They may well have invented round ones before you
were born. I first saw some in use in 1977, for air
traffic control purposes, but it wouldn't be the
least bit surprising if the very first softcopy
monitors used for computers, back in the early
1950s(?) were round; it's a lot easier to handle the
stresses on a CRT tube if it is rotationally
identical to itself.

Here, for example, is a screenshot of Spacewar, at
MIT in 1961, arguably (most people credit it that
way) the first softcopy computer graphics game.


http://www.cs.queensu.ca/home/jstewart/454/images/history/spacewar-fig3.jpeg

Notice particularly the non-rectangular screen.

Thus my argument that the current shape of the
NetHack dungeon level is an artifact of a particular
technology, mostly superseded by now (though we
still maintain ports to MS-DOS and its default 25x80
screen), and in no wise essential or appropriate
to the game.

